<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Network security policy for the Aria softphone.

    Effective from API 24+ (this app's minSdk is 26, so the policy always
    applies). On API 24+ this file OVERRIDES the manifest's
    android:usesCleartextTraffic flag, which we still set to "false" as
    defense-in-depth for any tooling/older-behaviour that reads it.

    Policy:
      * base-config denies cleartext for ALL destinations by default. This
        covers every connection that goes through the platform HTTP stack —
        OkHttp / HttpURLConnection (see service/PbxApiClient.kt) and the
        Rust reqwest client — so the gateway (https://push.ariaroute.com) and
        any API traffic can only ever be reached over TLS. A hostile QR that
        tries to point the client at an http:// gateway will now fail to
        connect instead of leaking the token in cleartext.
      * We keep the system trust anchors only (no user-added CAs), so a
        locally installed / MITM CA cannot silently intercept app TLS.

    SIP is NOT affected by this policy. SIP signalling and RTP media are sent
    over RAW UDP/TCP sockets opened by the native Rust SIP stack (via the
    UniFFI bridge), not through Android's managed HTTP stack.
    networkSecurityConfig only governs connections made through
    HttpsURLConnection / HttpURLConnection / the platform socket-trust APIs;
    it does NOT inspect or block arbitrary raw DatagramSocket / Socket
    traffic. Denying cleartext here therefore does not break UDP/TCP SIP
    registration or calling. (Media confidentiality comes from
    SDES-SRTP, which aria-mobile-core now offers on every call. SIP
    signalling itself is NOT confidential by default: transport defaults to
    "udp" at every call site and the QR payload sets it without an
    allow-list. This comment previously claimed both were handled; neither
    was.)

    The domain-config below re-permits cleartext ONLY for loopback / the
    Android-emulator host alias, so a developer can point the app at a local
    plaintext dev backend without weakening the production policy. These hosts
    are not routable off-device and never reachable in a shipped build.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!-- Dev/local only: localhost and the emulator's host-loopback alias. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>

</network-security-config>
